+# Design
+
+## Context
+
+`src/json-pointer.ts` parses RFC 6901 pointers (private `parsePointer`) and exposes `resolvePointer`, which throws on invalid or unresolvable pointers. Patch operations need the parsed tokens, not only resolution. The library has no runtime dependencies and tests run with `node --test`.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:**
+- RFC 6902 behaviour for all six operations, atomic from the caller's view.
+- No mutation of inputs; no shared structure between inputs and result.
+
+**Non-Goals:**
+- JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7396), patch generation (diff), URI fragment pointers.
+- Validation that the document is JSON (functions, `undefined`, class instances are not checked).
+
+## Decisions
+
+- **Deep-clone the document once, then mutate the clone in place.** Operations are simple in-place edits; an exception discards the clone, giving atomicity. Alternative, copy-on-write along the path, is more efficient but more complex; documents are small. Values from `value`/`from` are also cloned when inserted, so the result never aliases the patch.
+- **Own clone helper** (recursive, not `structuredClone`) so that member names such as `__proto__` are created as own properties (via `Object.defineProperty`) rather than invoking the prototype setter; own-property checks use `Object.hasOwn`.
+- **Export `parsePointer` from `json-pointer.ts`** (not from `index.ts`) for reuse, so pointer syntax rules stay in one place.
+- **Locate parent and last token**, then dispatch on parent type (array/object/scalar). `add`, `remove`, `replace` share this. Array indexes use the same strict `0|[1-9][0-9]*` rule as `resolvePointer`; `-` is only valid for `add`.
+- **move = remove then add**, after checking that `from` is not a proper token-prefix of `path`; equal `from`/`path` is a no-op (after checking `from` exists).
+- **Removing or moving the root** is rejected with an error, since the root has no parent to remove it from. `replace`/`add` at the root replace the document.
+- **test uses a recursive deep-equal** with order-insensitive objects; `Object.is` is not used (so `0` equals `-0`, as JSON numbers).
+- **Validation per operation just before applying it**, so errors are thrown as `Error` with the operation index in the message. The patch shape (array) is checked up front. Which invalid operation is reported does not matter since the whole call throws.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- [Full clone cost on large documents] → acceptable for the intended use; copy-on-write can come later without changing behaviour.
+- [Deep recursion on very deep documents] → same limits as `JSON.stringify`; accepted.
